title: The metafolder API
tags: [[Writing a panel type]]
kind: reference
audience: dev
aliases: metafolder-api panel-api api.ts __ui.js panel-shim
summary: The object a panel's mount receives — the daemon, the change feed, workspace variables, commands and keys, the file system, the status bar — and the helper modules served beside it.

`mount(root, metafolder)` receives one object per panel instance, built by
`createPanelApi` (`frontend/src/lib/panels/api.ts`) and typed by
`frontend/types/metafolder-api.d.ts`, which declares `MetafolderApi` globally so
that a panel's JSDoc can name it without an import. Every method calls a command of
the Rust side directly. A [[user command|User commands]] receives the same object
as `mf`, less what only means something inside a panel.

!! The panel itself

|!Member |!Is |
|`workspaceId`, `panelType` |the instance's workspace and type |
|`guiServer` |the GUI server's base URL, `http://127.0.0.1:<gui-port>`, for `/fsraw`, `/thumbnail`, `/document` |
|`sessionToken` |the token those routes want as `?token=` in a URL given to an `<img>` or a `<video>` ([[Session tokens]]) |
|`pageSize` |this type's entry of `[page-size]` in `config.toml`, or `undefined` |
|`settings` |the `[panels]` table, frozen, keys in camelCase: `finderDebounceMs`, `livePreviewDebounceMs`, `taskPollMs`… each possibly `undefined` |
|`defaults` |this type's `[panel-defaults.<type>]` table, frozen, keys in camelCase (`text-preview-limit` reads as `textPreviewLimit`); a key the user did not set is `undefined` ([[GUI configuration]]) |
|`visible` |whether the panel is on screen now |
|`whenVisible(fn)` |runs `fn` now when visible, else once at the next display; the same function armed several times runs once |
|`onVisibility(listener)` |`listener(visible, slot)` at each change; `slot` is `left`, `right` or `null` |
|`ready` |an already resolved promise, kept for panels that awaited it |

!! `daemon`

The daemon's HTTP API, through the Rust proxy ([[Connection to the daemon]]). Paths
are the daemon's own ([[Query endpoints]], [[Metarecord endpoints]]).

|!Method |!Does |
|`request(method, path, body?, opts?)` |one round-trip; resolves to `{status, body}` whatever the status |
|`call(method, path, body?, opts?)` |the same, but returns the body and throws the daemon's `error` message on a status of 400 or more |
|`query(repo, body, opts?)` |one page of `POST /query`: `{uuids, records, nextCursor, total}` (`total` is `null` unless the body asked for it) |
|`metarecords(repo, uuids)` |the named metarecords that exist, as a `Map` by uuid — one `uuid_in` query |
|`treePaths(repo, field, uuids)` |`{uuid: [path]}`: each one's positions in a `tree_ref` field, `[]` for one without any |
|`fields(repo)` |the field catalogue, `[{name, type}]` |
|`resolvePath(repo, uuid)` |the repository-relative path of a metarecord's `mfr_path` |
|`resolveTreeRef(repo, {parent, name}, field?)` |the path a `tree_ref` value names (`field` defaults to `mfr_path`) |
|`metarecordPaths(repo, metarecord)` |its absolute paths on this machine |
|`repoRoot(repo)`, `repoInternalDir(repo)` |the repository's root and its `.metafolder/internal/`, from `GET /repos` (kept per instance once read) |
|`initRepo({root, name?, metafolder?, noIgnore?, ignore?})` |creates a repository ''and'' applies its ignore presets, as `mf repo init` does; a bare `POST /repos/init` would skip them |
|`setContext(text)` |declares what the user asked for (a query's text, 200 characters at most); it is sent with the panel's later calls and written to the [[Slow log]] when one is slow. `null` clears it |

Nothing here is kept between two calls, paths included
([[Daemon data in panels]], [[TreeRef path conventions]]).

''Aborting a read.'' `request`, `call` and `query` take `{signal}` (an
`AbortSignal`) as `opts`. Aborting drops the call in flight and rejects the promise
with an `AbortError`, as `fetch` does. The daemon sees the connection close and
cancels the query instead of finishing it for nobody ([[Query limits]]) — which
matters because a repository answers one operation at a time: the count of a query
the user already replaced would hold the next page behind it. `metarecord-list`
aborts its page and its count at each new query. The abort and the call are two
separate commands, so the abort may reach the proxy first: it is then kept for 30
seconds and the call is dropped on arrival, never sent.

!! `changes`

The change feed ([[Daemon data in panels]]).

* `subscribe(({repo, uuids}) => …)` — called for each change the feed sees; `uuids` are the metarecords touched, or `null` for "the whole repository". Returns the function that unsubscribes: return it from `mount`.
* `sync(repo)` — polls now, on top of the background poll.

!! `query`

Text transformations run in the GUI's Rust side by the shared engine, with no
daemon round-trip ([[Why expansion runs in the client]]). Each rejects on a parse
error.

* `parse(dsl)` — DSL text to the query's JSON form.
* `expand(text)` — simplified text to DSL text.
* `grammarSource()` — the simplified grammar's source, for the help.

!! `workspace`

The [[workspace variables|Workspace variables]] of the instance's workspace.

* `get(key)`, `set(key, value)` — both return promises.
* `all()` — every variable the workspace holds now, as an object, `active_repo` included: what a panel built after some were set starts from, since `onChange` only reports what moves afterwards.
* `onChange(key, listener)` — `listener(value)` at each change of `key`; with the key `'*'`, `listener(value, key)` for every variable.
* `adoptRepo(repo)` — sets `active_repo` on a workspace that has none yet; a workspace never changes repository afterwards ([[Workspaces]]).

!! `commands`, `invoke`, `addKeybinding`

```
metafolder.commands.register('my-panel:set-mode', {
  label: 'My panel: change the display mode',
  args: [{ name: 'mode', prompt: () => 'Mode?', complete: () => ['list', 'grid'] }],
  handler: (mode) => setMode(mode),
});
metafolder.addKeybinding('my-panel:set-mode list', 'ctrl+l');
metafolder.addKeybinding('my-panel:open-form', 'ctrl+e', { textInput: true });
await metafolder.invoke('panel:split');
```

|!Option of `register` |!Meaning |
|`label` |shown beside the name in the command input (default: the name) |
|`handler` |receives the arguments, as strings; may be async; what it throws is shown in the status bar |
|`args` |the declared arguments, asked for when missing ([[Interactive command arguments]], [[Completion views]]) |
|`reveal` |`true` puts the panel on screen before running ([[Writing a panel type]]) |
|`log` |`false` keeps the invocations out of the message log — for a command run at each key press |

A command of this name defined in `commands.js` is dropped in favour of the
panel's ([[User commands]]). Where a key applies is said by the binding, not by
the command.

`addKeybinding(invocation, combo, {when?, textInput?, focus?})` ''suggests'' a key:
it is applied unless `keybindings.toml` already binds that combo in that scope, and
it is not written to the file ([[Changing keybindings]]). `when` defaults to the
panel's type — the key applies while a panel of that type has the focus; the other
two are those of [[Keybindings]]. `combo` may be a sequence (`g r`).

`commands.invoke(invocation)` runs a command line as if typed, and
`metafolder.invoke` is the same function. `commands.keybindings()` resolves to the
compiled key table as it stands — `[{keys, invocation, when, text_input, focus}]` —
which is what the help fills its key hints from.

!! `fs`

The disk, through the Rust side; the daemon is not involved, so a tracked file
changed here is recorded when the watcher sees it or at the next reconcile.

|!Method |!Does |
|`readDir(path)` |`[{name, path, is_dir, escaped}]` |
|`stat(path)` |`{path, is_dir, size, mtime}`, following symbolic links; `mtime` in milliseconds |
|`exists(path)` |whether anything is there, a broken link included |
|`homeDir()` |the user's home directory |
|`mkdir(path)` |one directory, whose parent must exist |
|`createFile(path)` |an empty file |
|`move(from, to)` |a rename, falling back to copy and delete across file systems |
|`copy(from, to)` |a file or a whole tree |
|`remove(path)` |a permanent deletion, of a tree too |

None of them overwrites: `mkdir`, `createFile`, `move` and `copy` fail when the
destination exists.

''A path is a handle.'' The frontend speaks JSON, so a path it holds is text, while
a path on disk is bytes that need not be UTF-8. Every path crossing this boundary
is in the `%XX` form of [[Tree names]]: itself when it is text, `/dir/caf%E9.mp4`
when it holds a byte no text can represent. A panel slices and joins it like a
plain path and hands it back unchanged — to `fs`, or as `?path=` to `/fsraw`,
`/thumbnail`, `/document` — and the Rust side turns it back into the exact bytes.
A handle that spells an escape has two readings; the one that exists on disk is
taken, the literal one first, and a path that exists under neither is taken
literally, so creating `new%file.txt` creates exactly that. `readDir` marks with
`escaped: true` an entry whose name needed escaping.

!! Other members

|!Member |!Is |
|`statusBar.message(text, timeoutMs?)`, `statusBar.error(error, timeoutMs?)` |a line in the workspace's status bar; `error` takes what a `catch` caught ([[Status bar and task bar]]) |
|`messages.list()`, `messages.append(text)`, `messages.onAppend(listener)` |the workspace's message log; the listener receives `null` when the log is cleared |
|`contextMenu(event, items)` |opens the HTML menu at the event's position; an item is `{label, action?, disabled?}`, a header or `'-'`. Returns nothing: the chosen item runs its `action` ([[Context menus]]) |
|`contextMenu.addDefaultItems(event => items)` |adds items to the default menu when it opens inside this panel |
|`pick.start(spec)` |opens a picker workspace; the answer comes back as the `pick_result` variable, matched by the `token` of the spec ([[Value picker]]). `createPickRunner` of `/__value-widget.js` wraps it |
|`config.refSeed(field)`, `config.labelSeparator()` |the `[ref-seeds]` rule of a field and the separator of a completion label ([[Ref value seeds]]) |
|`history.read(repo, zone)`, `history.append(repo, zone, entry)` |the store behind `attachHistory` of `/__history.js` ([[Input history]]) |
|`recent.list(repo, limit?)`, `recent.touch(repo, uuid)` |the recently viewed metarecords |
|`trash.list`, `restore`, `remove`, `empty`, `trashPath` |the repository's trash ([[Trash]]) |
|`log.rollback(repo, target)`, `log.revert(repo, target, withDependents)` |navigation of the event log, files included ([[Filesystem coordination]]) |
|`orphans.detect()`, `count()`, `delete()` |the orphaned metarecords of the workspace's repository ([[Orphans in the GUI]]) |
|`ignore.presets()`, `current`, `apply`, `write` |ignore presets ([[Setting ignore patterns]]) |
|`sync.status`, `link`, `unlink`, `plan`, `run`, `show` |synchronisation between two repositories |
|`bench.measure(name, fn)`, `bench.record(name, ms)` |times a phase for the profiling harness; every daemon call is timed already, as `mf:daemon <method> <path>` |

These go through the Rust side rather than the daemon because they are either not
the daemon's business — the history, the recent list, the trash are files the GUI
manages itself ([[GUI design decisions]]) — or an orchestration shared with `mf`.

!! Helper modules

The GUI server serves, beside the panels, modules any panel may import by an
absolute path: `import { el, byId } from '/__ui.js'`. Their sources are in
`crates/gui/panel-shim/`.

|!Module |!Gives |
|`/__ui.js` |`el(tag, props, ...children)`, `byId`, `qs`, `qsa`; `formatValue`, `valueEl` for a field value; `field`, `fields`, `byName` to read a metarecord's fields; `thumbnail`, `fileTypeGlyph`; `messageOf`, `formatSize`, `formatAge` |
|`/__menu.js`, `/__select.js` |the HTML menu, and a drop-down built on it in place of the native `<select>` |
|`/__file-actions.js` |cut, copy, paste, rename, trash on a row, with one clipboard for the whole GUI, and the shared context menu |
|`/__multi-select.js` |the checked selection ([[Cross-panel selection]]) |
|`/__find-entry.js` |`registerFind`: a panel's `<panel>:find` command ([[Jumping to an entry]]) |
|`/__history.js` |`attachHistory` on a text field |
|`/__paged-list.js`, `/__coalesce.js` |loading a long list by pages; `latestOnly`, for an effect asked for faster than it runs |
|`/__value-widget.js`, `/__schema-template.js`, `/__completions.js` |typed value inputs and the picker runner; the user schema, read once per repository; the candidate rules of a completion |
|`/__columns.js`, `/__finder.js` |the column and finder syntaxes of `metarecord-list` |
|`/__orphan.js`, `/__mounts.js`, `/__watched.js`, `/__activity.js`, `/__ignore.js` |what a row says about its file: orphaned, on an unmounted volume, watched or not, how noisy, ignored |
|`/__watcher-settle.js` |`createCatchup`: re-reading after the panel changed the disk itself, timed from the daemon's quiet period ([[Event batching]]) |
|`/__keyhints.js`, `/__help.js` |the help panel's key hints and page lookup |

`el` builds DOM without `innerHTML` — values come from the user's files, and text
stays text. `class` takes a string or an array whose falsy entries are dropped, a
key starting with `on` attaches a listener, a key that is a property of the element
is assigned, and any other becomes an attribute. Children are flattened, and
`null`, `undefined` and `false` are skipped, so `cond && el(…)` can be written
inline.
